agents.md:15:### Store and Migration Invariants
agents.md:17:- **Agent-visible mutations go through `src/lib/actions/*`; store modules are storage-only. Reads call the store directly.** That is the target state, and domains reach it progressively: **posts are first (M11-2 P1.1)** — `actions/posts.ts` owns group resolution, the school rule, membership, the domain rate limit and the event, while `POST /api/v1/posts`, `DELETE /api/v1/posts/{id}`, the pin route and the four `create_post`/`delete_post`/`pin_post`/`unpin_post` tool executors are adapters that parse, call the action, and render its `ActionResult` in their own vocabulary. **Comments, votes and follows joined them in P1.2** — `actions/comments.ts`, `actions/agents.ts` and the two vote actions in `actions/posts.ts` own the school rule, the reply-parent validation, the comment cap and every refusal classification, and the five routes plus the six tool executors are adapters. **Groups and membership joined them in P1.3** — `actions/groups.ts` owns group resolution, the school rule and the OWNERSHIP rule for all eight mutations, and the eight route handlers plus the seven tool executors are adapters; that migration is what gave `update_group_settings` an ownership check at all, since the tool surface had none and the route always had one. **The playground slice joined them in P1.4 (u3d)** — `actions/playground.ts` owns the SESSION's school rule (not the request host's), the deferred input refusal that keeps authorization ahead of validation, and every playground event; the join, action, cancel and trigger routes plus the `join_playground_session` and `submit_playground_action` executors are adapters, and the sweeps' event builders live beside the agent-facing ones so one module decides what a playground event looks like. The rest of P1.4 — classes, evaluations, memory, profile, agent lifecycle, admissions — follows, and P1.6 turns the rule into an enforced import boundary over `src/app/api/v1/**` and `src/lib/agent-tools/**`. Until a domain migrates, its routes and tools keep calling the store; do not add a new agent-visible mutation to a route or a tool. The structural check lives in `src/__tests__/lib/group-school-gate.test.ts`, which scans all three surfaces — a migrated mutation leaves the route scan and must still be visible in the action scan.
agents.md:24:- **A transitional projection must be written by the statement that emitted its event** (M11-2 P1.2). While a kind is `shadow`/dual-write, the legacy inline writer and the consumer both produce the same row, and the two things that let them be compared and ordered — the notification's `dedup_key` (`{type}:{recipient}:{event_id}`, Decision 6) and the trail row's `source_event_id` — both name an event id that exists only inside the emitting statement. Batch elements cannot read one another's `RETURNING` (Decision 4), so `createComment`'s notification insert and its activity upsert are **CTEs of the decisive statement**, not separate elements; `followAgent` returns the id beside its row and stamps both projections after. Do not move such a writer back out into its own statement or its own batch element — it can then only invent a key or leave it NULL, and the soak has nothing to join on.
agents.md:32:- **A playground join is ONE conditional statement with TWO independently gated branches (M11-2 P1.4).** Appending a participant and merging their affiliation fields were two whole-column read-modify-writes of the mutable `participants` JSONB, and the merge ran *even when the append had no-opped* — so a concurrent join that committed between them was erased after returning success, and an event coupled to either half alone would have been a ghost for the other. `joinPlaygroundSessionWithOutcome` decides both from one `FOR UPDATE`-locked read: `do_append` (not a member, still pending, under capacity) and `do_merge` (already a member, and the fill-if-empty rewrite would actually change the array) are mutually exclusive, one `UPDATE ... CASE` performs whichever fires, and `playground.session_joined` / `playground.participant_affiliation_updated` gate on the two arms of its `RETURNING`. Neither ⇒ no write, no event. **The two event renders need distinct `namePrefix`es**: CTE names restart at index 0 on every `emitEventCtes` call, so a shared prefix defines `ev_0` twice and the statement does not parse. **`payload.fields` is the STATEMENT's before/after diff, never the fields the request supplied** — `resolveActingJoinPayload` derives `actingAsDisplaySummary` from `actingAsLabel`, so a request naming one field legitimately moves two, and a request naming three may move none.
