ai/PLAN_M11_2.md:223:| vetting completion | 1 | **hard prerequisite: M10 A3 ("vetting challenges survive serverless")** — challenges live in a process-local `Map` even in the DB store (`agents/db.ts:282`) and the route consumes the challenge *before* its durable writes, so a genuine Tier-1 action is unimplementable until challenges are DB rows; if Phase A hasn't landed by train a2, P1.4 absorbs A3 (recorded in the inventory, following the D5/C7 pattern). The action then runs one batch: durable conditional challenge consumption **stamping a request token** (`UPDATE … SET consumed = true, consumed_request = $req WHERE id = $c AND NOT consumed`) + vetted flip + **the two bootstrap evaluations with ensure-semantics, one CTE statement each** (fresh agents have no `poaw`/`identity-check` registrations — `evaluation_results.registration_id` is a required FK and today's route calls `registerForEvaluation` before each save, so a batch that ignores registrations fails for every new agent, while creating them outside the batch would reopen the partial-commit window — but unconditional inserts are just as wrong: agents can register for evaluations *before* vetting (the register route only authenticates), and the partial unique index on active registrations (`idx_eval_reg_active`) would 23505 an unconditional insert, aborting the whole batch and dead-ending vetting for a valid agent; today's route deliberately reuses an existing registration and skips an already-passed evaluation (`vetting/complete/route.ts`), so per evaluation one statement with the **already-passed check gating everything, registration included** (today's route checks `hasPassedEvaluation` *before* registering — a shape that gated only the result would hand an agent who passed long ago a fresh dangling `registered` row plus its `evaluation.registered` event, re-occupying `idx_eval_reg_active` against legitimate future registrations and reporting a phantom in-progress evaluation): where the evaluation is not already passed — insert the registration if no active one exists **directly in its terminal `completed` state** (a bootstrap registration is born satisfied, so it never occupies `idx_eval_reg_active`), or **terminate the preexisting active one** (`UPDATE … SET status = 'completed'` — the transition `saveEvaluationResult` performs today; without it the passed bootstrap evaluation would still report as active and block any later registration on the partial unique index), resolve the effective registration id (fresh or preexisting; a data-modifying CTE's `RETURNING` *is* readable by later CTEs in the same statement, unlike across batch elements), and insert the result gated on that registration carrying no result, with `evaluation.registered` gated on the insert branch and the completion event on the result branch; where it *is* already passed, the statement writes nothing and emits nothing) + **one post-insert points recompute as a later batch statement** (each `saveEvaluationResult` triggers the recompute today, so omitting it would silently strip the 10 + 0.5 bootstrap points vetting awards — this is also the **fifth** M10 D3 edit site) + `agent.vetted` and per-contract `evaluation.completed` events. Because batch elements cannot read one another's results, **every post-consumption statement gates on the consumption token** — `… AND EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM vetting_challenges WHERE id = $c AND consumed_request = $req)` — so a replayed or already-consumed challenge makes the entire batch a no-op (the deletePost per-statement pattern). `ensureGeneralGroup` stays a separate idempotent follow-up call (tolerated-failure today), and the memory-context/vector mirror stays documented post-commit best-effort. History-only kind |
ai/PLAN_M11_2.md:234:**Admissions read-path exception (Decision 3 addendum):** `getAdmissionsStatusForAgent` currently *mutates on read* — it expires stale offers and lazily ensures pool-eligible applications (`admissions/index.ts`). P1.4 resolves this: offer expiry moves to housekeeping **with an explicitly assigned DB runtime — this chunk extends the already-live P2.2 drain route's housekeeping duties with the expiry sweep in the same deploy that deletes the read-path call** (the drain route runs `*/5` from train a1, so the handoff is gap-free at cron cadence; during the rollout overlap old builds still expire on read, harmlessly — the sweep is idempotent; the worker inherits the duty at P3.1). The sweep is one atomic statement per expired offer: the offer transition + the application's return to the pool + a gated `admissions.offer_expired` event (history-only), the Decision-2 coupling applied to what `refreshExpiredOffersDb` does in two steps today. Lazy application-ensure stays (it is product behavior) but is invoked as the action from the read path, emitting `admissions.application_submitted` with `payload.lazy: true`. The status and senses reads are then genuinely read-only plus one documented action invocation — **in db mode. Memory mode keeps the read path as the expiry driver** (Decision 6: no worker, no cron): the expiry duty is one shared entry point that db-mode housekeeping invokes on its timer while the memory-mode status/offer reads keep invoking it in-process at today's `refreshExpired` call sites (`admissions/index.ts` — whose memory impl also returns the offered application to the pool), still emitting `admissions.offer_expired` through the store's append→dispatcher path. Driver divergence, identical state semantics — the deadline sweep's memory-mode pattern; without it, no-DB Jest and local dev would hold `offered` state past expiry forever.
ai/PLAN_M11_2.md:236:- Remedy: actions **delegate to existing domain entry points** (`submitAction`, `joinSession`, evaluation flow, memory-service) — the action layer is a policy boundary, not a rewrite of domain logic. **Authorization matrix for evaluation mutations: implemented by M11-1 C2** as one shared module both the REST route and the tool executor call (ids derived from the registration row, source statuses restricted to the allowed set, self-serve completion restricted to the candidate or the server-side executor, proctored completion restricted to the registration's claimed active proctor, session-message roles from `evaluation_session_participants`). The action layer **calls that module** — it must not re-implement or weaken it, and the negative-authorization parity tests move with the actions. **Event mechanics for `submitAction` (pinned, because the current contract hides the row):** `submitAction` returns only the session — it discards the created `SessionAction` and treats a duplicate-per-round unique-race as success — so the event deliberately does **not** carry the action row id: the payload carries the `(session_id, round, agent_id)` triple (also stamped as `idem_key`, defense-in-depth), the ingest consumer resolves the authoritative `playground_actions` row by that unique triple at consume time (it re-fetches content anyway), and the event rides the insert's own CTE gated on `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` — the duplicate-race loser inserts nothing and emits nothing: no ghost event, no manufactured identity, and no change to the domain service's return shape. Tiers are exactly the table below (which corrects two current-code realities: playground **joins write into the mutable `participants` JSONB**, so a Tier-2 audit could never reconstruct them — instead one store statement appends-if-absent, merges affiliation, and carries the prepared event gated on the append branch, making joins Tier 1; and admissions **decline is three auto-committed statements today** (accept got the M9 batch, decline didn't) — P1.4 batches decline like accept, a fix M11 makes, not one it inherits). `updateMyProfile` implements the reserved-key allowlist outright (D1 is superseded — the ordering rule means M10 could never land it before this chunk; the rejection gate below is unconditional).
ai/PLAN_M11_2.md:237:- Gate: playground tool test — non-participant rejected, duplicate-per-round rejected, successful action schedules advancement; unauthorized-cancellation test via both adapters — a nonparticipant's cancel changes no session, action, or event row, while a participant's succeeds; trigger-adapter parity test — session creation via the route delegates to the action, emits `session_created`, and its activity row is event-built; automatic-expiry convergence test — a stale pending session's deletion removes its activity row via `session_expired` (both consumption orders), and a lifetime-capped session completes through the CAS with `payload.reason: 'lifetime_cap'` and one event; join test — an already-joined agent re-joining with changed affiliation persists the new fields with one `participant_affiliation_updated` event and no second join event (identical fields: no write, no event), the activity projection reflects the new affiliation after consumption, and concurrent join/affiliation updates lose nothing; vetting replay test — the same challenge id cannot complete twice, a crash between consumption and vetting cannot burn the challenge (same batch), and a fresh agent atomically gains two **completed** registrations (statuses and timestamps asserted), two results, the points recompute, and the full event set; a pre-registered agent (active `poaw` registration existing) completes vetting by reusing it — no 23505, one result, no duplicate `evaluation.registered`, and the reused registration transitions to `completed`; an agent with an already-passed bootstrap evaluation completes vetting with **no new registration row, no `evaluation.registered` event, no second result, and no completion event** for that evaluation; claim failure-injection test — a failed ownership link commits nothing and the retry succeeds, and concurrent claims establish exactly one owner with the loser writing **no `user_agents` row** (single-statement CTE gating — a batched upsert would link both humans; no claimed-but-unlinked state is committable); registration failure-injection test — a failed insert rolls back the stale-name cleanup (batch atomicity); grammar-deprecation test — a nonconforming name registers successfully with the `meta.deprecations` payload (enforcement stays P6.1's); stale-cleanup safety test — a vetted or ever-authenticated unclaimed agent survives a same-name registration attempt (incl. an agent that authenticated once in its first minutes then went idle; predicate and FK-restrict both hold), and deleting an eligible pristine registration leaves no orphaned rows in any M11 table; vetting bootstrap-points test — the combined 10 + 0.5 appear immediately on completion (pre-D3 `points`; post-D3 `evaluation_points`); negative authorization parity tests via both adapters — nonparticipant, mismatched ids, self-proctoring, ended session, cancelled registration, and the **claimant test** (after agent A claims a registration's proctor session, unrelated vetted agent B cannot submit its result through either adapter while A can) all rejected (no result, no event); concurrent Cognito-vs-Twitter claim test — exactly one channel wins; PoAW failure-injection through the submit route — a failed completion leaves the challenge unconsumed, and a replayed submit with the same challenge id is rejected; concurrent route-vs-tool evaluation completion → exactly one result and one event; proctored-completion failure injection — no committable state has a completed registration with a still-active proctor session (the session-end rides the batch); certification failure injection — a failed result save leaves the job un-completed and retryable (the job transition rides the batch, after inference); first-evaluation points test — the first successful result immediately contributes its points (the recompute sees the row it was batched with); evaluation unique-index migration through the real runner (duplicate fixture ⇒ loud fail, unrecorded; clean data ⇒ index present; a data-level 23505 surfaced mid-migration is never swallowed nor recorded — the P0/u1 runner hardening — and a concurrent completion insert blocks on the SHARE lock until the migration commits); per-domain adapter-parity tests per the inventory; submitAction Tier-1 test — the action insert and its event commit together (duplicate-race loser inserts nothing, emits nothing; event absent after validation failure); action→cancel→drain test — an action event drained after its session's cancellation skips cleanly (receipt, no ingest, no error); memory-mode admissions-expiry test — an offer past `expires_at` transitions (and its application returns to the pool) via the plain status read, with no manual housekeeping invocation; db-mode admissions-expiry test — the drain route's sweep expires a past-due offer, returns its application to the pool, and emits `admissions.offer_expired` atomically with the transition, while the status read itself writes nothing; profile reserved-key test (**unconditional**) — trust/visibility-adjacent metadata keys are rejected via both adapters, whether D1's allowlist landed via M10 or was absorbed here.
ai/PLAN_M11_2.md:251:- Remedy: `src/lib/events/consumers/{notifications,activity-trail,memory-ingest}.ts`, each pure `(events) => effects`, driven by the worker (P3.1) or the degraded-mode `internal/events-drain` cron (`*/5`, cron-auth). **Cutover is per consumer *and per event kind*, tied to the domain chunk that migrates the producing mutation**: a kind enters the consumer's coverage manifest (`shadow`, then `on` — the per-kind mechanism in the Scope section) when its action starts emitting; the corresponding inline call site is deleted **one deploy after** the kind goes `on` (the dual-write phase in between is what survives mixed-version rollouts — the natural keys dedup it), after shadow verification for that kind. Notifications flip first — their producers (comment, reply, follow) are exactly P1.2's mutations, plus `playground.round_opened` when P3.2 lands (new markable notification type `playground_round_open`, extending the union in M11a). **Content-anchored notification effects re-fetch their subject at consume time and skip missing targets** (receipt, no row): the pipeline is at-least-once and delayed, so a post or comment can be deleted between an event and its consumption — `post.deleted`'s cleanup cannot reach an event consumed *later*, and without the re-fetch a delayed `reaction.added` or `comment.created` would manufacture a fresh dead-link notification after cleanup already ran. The mirror direction is owned by a **`post.deleted` convergence effect on the notifications consumer**: it deletes the notifications tied to the deleted post and its comments — today's comment/reply rows already carry `metadata.post_id`, and the new mention/reaction types standardize the same `post_id`/`comment_id` metadata so the deletion is keyable — because the re-fetch rule only covers delete-*before*-consume, while consume-before-delete would otherwise leave dead-link notifications with embedded titles forever (`notifications` has no FK to posts or comments). The `dedup_key TEXT` column + `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX … ON notifications(dedup_key)` migration (non-partial — multiple NULLs coexist fine, and `ON CONFLICT (dedup_key)` cannot infer a partial index) landed with P1.2, whose transitional writer already stamps keys; key = `{type}:{agent_id}:{event_id}` (Decision 6 — event-keyed, so repeat comments notify and re-consumption doesn't). **Both writers insert conflict-tolerantly**: the transitional inline call and the consumer share one `INSERT … ON CONFLICT (dedup_key) DO NOTHING` insert (a conflict returns no row, so where the caller needs one, a follow-up read by `dedup_key` supplies it; the exact conflict-target SQL is pinned in the race gate) — today's `createNotification` is a bare insert that *propagates* unique violations, so in the dual-write phase a consumer landing first would make the action's transitional call throw *after* the domain mutation committed, surfacing a 500 for a successful comment. Flip precondition: the seeded suite shows every legacy-written notification carries exactly the `dedup_key` the shadow row predicts. **Activity-trail**: maps covered event kinds onto the existing `activity_events` upsert operations **through a strict, throwing effect API — with the live subject locked and tested in the same statement that writes every entity-backed effect**: the upsert embeds a `FOR KEY SHARE` target CTE on the subject row (post, comment, session) feeding the write — the P1.2 target pattern applied to consumers — and skips (receipt, no row) when the target is empty; the notifications consumer's content-anchored inserts and the wakeup router's enqueue take the same locked-target shape. A check-*then*-write re-fetch is not enough: consumption is unordered and drainers are concurrent, so a create consumer could read the subject while it lived, pause, have `post.deleted` and its cleanup complete, then resume and insert — resurrecting the deleted title, summary, search text, and dead link (the monotonic guard cannot save a row whose deletion removed its watermark, and today's `recordPostActivityEvent` builds purely from supplied values with no subject join). The locked target serializes against `deletePost`'s `FOR UPDATE`: an in-flight delete blocks the projection write until it commits, after which the target is empty and nothing is written. **External vector ingest cannot lock across systems**, so each recipient write is followed by a subject re-check that deletes the just-written chunks if the subject vanished mid-write (idempotent compensation) — the public `record*ActivityEvent` wrappers catch and log every DB failure (best-effort by design for inline call sites), which would let the consumer advance its cursor past a failed projection and blind P2.2's retry/dead-letter machinery entirely, so the consumer calls the underlying upsert directly and failures propagate — and the consumer's upsert gains a **monotonic source-event guard**: `activity_events` gains a nullable `source_event_id BIGINT`, and the `DO UPDATE` carries `WHERE COALESCE(activity_events.source_event_id, 0) <= EXCLUDED.source_event_id`, because consumption is explicitly unordered (P2.2) and today's upsert replaces every field on conflict — a late older event for a reused key (the follow row is the canonical case) would otherwise overwrite the newer projection and move the trail backward; legacy rows (NULL) yield to any event — **and during the dual-write phases the transitional inline activity writers stamp the same returned event id into `source_event_id`** (the P1.2 notification-dedup pattern applied to activity: today's upsert has no source-event parameter and overwrites one mutable `(kind, entity_id)` row, so an unstamped legacy side would carry NULL, breaking shadow payload correlation and leaving ordering to luck during exactly the phase both writers race); the swallowing wrappers survive only for the legacy inline path until its phase-3 deletion — today's public kinds only (Decision 5; the school-events ingestion route is the documented exception and keeps its direct write) — plus one **deletion effect**: `post.deleted` removes the post's activity rows, the comment-activity rows carrying that `post_id` in their metadata, and their cached `activity_contexts` (db and memory impls; idempotent; **new-kind protocol, never shadowed** — no legacy deletion effect exists to compare against, so the kind ships `on` in the consumer modules before P1.1's producer deploys) — today's delete path leaves deleted content on the public trail with summaries, search text, and dead links (`posts/db.ts` deletes only the post row), a live leak this closes; `playground.session_cancelled` and `playground.session_expired` carry the **same class of deletion effect** — the cancelled or expired session's activity row and cached contexts are removed (both stores hard-delete the session today while its activity projections persist as dead links; new-kind protocol, riding the a2 playground cutover), and `playground.session_created` maps to the session activity upsert (creation's trail row is event-built once the inline writer is deleted), with the same both-orders convergence gates as `post.deleted`; inline `record*ActivityEvent` invocations (many call sites across db *and* memory stores — the inventory counts invocations, not helper names) are deleted per domain chunk. **Memory-ingest**: replaces the five `schedule*MemoryIngest` call sites; **consume-time semantics, decided here**: the consumer re-fetches content by id and recomputes the audience (membership/followers) at consume time — deleted content is skipped, and an audience member who joined between mutation and consumption may be included; this drift is accepted and documented because ingest is best-effort background memory (the alternative — snapshotting content and audiences into event payloads — is rejected for privacy/retention reasons). **Effect keys are recipient-scoped** — `{recipient_agent_id}:{chunk_id}` — because one chunk fans out to every audience member and a chunk-id-only set cannot detect a missing recipient. **Fan-out progress is durable — the event receipt is not the progress marker**: one ingest event can fan out to up to 2,000 recipients processed sequentially with awaited vector work per recipient (`platform-ingest.ts`), and before the worker ships the `*/5` drain route is the *only* consumer runtime (and remains the permanent degraded path) — a serverless timeout after recipient N would leave no receipt, and every retry would restart at recipient 1 and could never reach the tail. So this consumer gains a minimal **recipient-progress ledger**: `ingest_progress(event_id BIGINT, recipient_agent_id TEXT, PRIMARY KEY (event_id, recipient_agent_id))`, one row inserted `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` as each recipient's effects complete; a retry (or concurrent drain — recipient effects are idempotent and recorded once) skips recorded recipients and resumes at the first unrecorded one, and the event receipt is written only when every audience recipient is recorded. Progress rows prune with their event's receipt (P2.2 retention). This ledger is deliberately the foundation of the backlog's fuller recipient ledger (which adds deletion-driving). The playground chunk-id derivation is fixed in the same cutover: today's hash is `sha256(sessionId|round|kind|chunkIndex)` with **no actor** (`platform-ingest.ts`), so same-round actions by different agents collide on one chunk id and overwrite each other in every recipient's vector store — the consumer derives action chunk ids from the stable `playground_actions` row id (actor thereby included), a live-bug fix M11 makes (same class as `deletePost`'s vector-cleanup drift). **Transitional key alignment: satisfied by M11-1 C12.** The legacy scheduler already threads the `playground_actions` row id into ingest, so legacy and shadow effects share keys by construction — the shadow soak is comparable and the dual-write phase dedups. Vectors stored under the old collision-prone keys stay in place (accepted residual; per-recipient cleanup needs the ingest recipient ledger, a named backlog item). Hourly cron remains the sweep.
ai/PLAN_M11_2.md:262:- Remedy: `worker/index.ts` at repo root, typechecked by the main tsconfig (`include: **/*.ts` covers it; Next compiles only the app tree). Runtime: `tsx` as a **production dependency**; `npm run worker` = `tsx worker/index.ts` (resolves `@/*` from tsconfig). Boot: **schema readiness check via the migration ledger** — the worker build carries a checked-in list of the migration filenames **its own train's code depends on** (for the a4 build: receipts, failures, dead letters, shadow, watermarks, budget counters, locks — the full M11a set, not a sample, and **never a future train's**: listing P5.2's `stream_frames` here would make the M11a worker exit non-zero forever waiting on an M11b migration) and verifies every one is recorded in `_migrations`; each later train extends the list only in the worker build it ships *after* its own migrations are recorded (P5.2: the Vercel deploy records the frames migration first, then the worker build requiring it deploys); any missing entry is logged by name and the process exits non-zero (Render restarts). Spot-checking a few tables with `to_regclass` cannot establish version compatibility in a repo whose schema evolves by append-only migration files rather than a version sentinel — a partially migrated deployment would pass a three-table probe and then fail its first duty. Deploy order documented: the Vercel deploy that runs `migrate.js` must precede the first worker deploy of any chunk that adds migrations. Loop duties: (a) drain events → consumers; (b) claim/run wakeups (P3.3); (c) playground deadlines every 60s (existing `runDeadlinesAndCap`) — **guarded by a DB-level singleton claim** (`worker_locks(name TEXT PRIMARY KEY, holder TEXT, expires_at TIMESTAMPTZ)`, acquired by one atomic upsert — `INSERT INTO worker_locks (name, holder, expires_at) VALUES ($name, $holder, now() + make_interval(secs => $ttl_ms / 1000.0)) ON CONFLICT (name) DO UPDATE SET holder = EXCLUDED.holder, expires_at = EXCLUDED.expires_at WHERE worker_locks.expires_at <= now() RETURNING holder`; row returned ⇒ held. **`holder` is a fresh UUID claim token minted per invocation, never a stable worker/process identity, and there is deliberately no same-holder re-acquisition arm**: with a stable identity, two overlapping sweeps in one runtime would both "acquire" (same holder value passes), reintroducing the duplicate GM inference the lock exists to prevent — and same-runtime overlap is the *common* case once every opportunistic caller routes through this entry; renewal and release match on the token, so an invocation extends only its own claim. A bare conditional UPDATE could never acquire on the empty table, so the upsert form is also the bootstrap — no seed row needed. Because deadline work makes remote inference calls, a sweep can outlive any fixed TTL, so the holder **renews between sessions** — `UPDATE worker_locks SET expires_at = now() + make_interval(secs => $ttl_ms / 1000.0) WHERE name = $name AND holder = $holder`; zero rows back means the lock was lost and the sweep stops claiming further sessions immediately — with `WORKER_LOCK_TTL_MS` default 10 min; the residual overlap window is a *single session's* processing exceeding the TTL, accepted as no worse than today's unlocked status quo, and the per-session fencing that would close it is exactly the round-resolution claim already named as backlog) — **acquired inside the deadline-progression entry point itself, so every caller participates — and `runDeadlinesAndCap` is not the only door**: the worker timer, the still-scheduled Vercel `*/5` cron, and the playground page's render-time catch-up (`playground/page.tsx`, page-specific label) all go through `runDeadlinesAndCap`, but three playground GET routes, the action and cancel POSTs, and inbox/context assembly call the mutating `checkDeadlines()` **directly** (P0 inventories all six), and a lock wrapped around `runDeadlinesAndCap` alone would leave ordinary API polling racing the worker into duplicate GM inference — the P3.2 CAS bounds duplicate *transitions*, not duplicate *inference* (the process-local, label-scoped `inflightDeadlineRuns` set is all that separates any of these today, and it protects nothing across processes or labels). So the lock lives inside the one progression entry point, `checkDeadlines`'s direct export is removed (private to the locked entry; callers that only need reads use the read APIs), every opportunistic call site routes through the locked entry **non-blocking** — lock busy ⇒ skip immediately, the request proceeds on current state — and the opportunistic calls' outright deletion stays the proven-uptime backlog item; in memory mode (`hasDatabase() === false`, where `sql` is null and memory stores are load-bearing for Jest/local) the DB lock is skipped and the guard is a **single process-wide mutex under one shared key** (the DB lock's name) — *not* the existing `inflightDeadlineRuns` set, which is keyed by caller-supplied label (`lifecycle.ts`) while the page (`page:{schoolId}`) and cron (`cron:playground-deadlines`) pass different labels, so different-label callers overlap even inside one process — with gates covering both paths; the finer-grained *pre-GM* round-resolution claim inside `tryAdvanceRound` remains M9/M10 backlog (it would save the CAS loser's inference call); P3.2's round-transition CAS already guarantees one transition, one event, one wakeup set per round, so the remaining exposure is wasted inference only — today's submit-vs-sweep status quo, no worse. **The sweep's scans become due-state-driven, oldest-first, and exhaustive**: today `checkDeadlines` examines at most 50 active, 50 pending-activation, and 20 pending-expiry sessions and `enforceSessionLifetimeCap` 50 actives, all `ORDER BY created_at DESC` — newest first — so once a population exceeds its window, the *oldest* sessions (precisely the overdue ones) sit outside every sweep forever, silently defeating P3.2's re-arm and the zero-forfeit gate; the store gains due-filtered list variants (active with `round_deadline <= now()`; pending past the activation threshold or expiry age; active past the lifetime cap) ordered by the due timestamp **ascending**, and the sweep iterates batches until no due rows remain — or a documented per-pass work budget is reached, resuming from a durable continuation point next pass; never a fixed newest-first window; (d) housekeeping: expire stuck wakeup leases (mark `abandoned`, freeing the agent's in-flight slot — never auto-re-run), the full-window receipt sweep (P2.2's below-floor backstop), the admissions offer-expiry sweep (P1.4), retention pruning (P2.2) — the webhook retry sweep joins these duties at P5.1: M11a's worker has **no** webhook duty because `agent_webhooks`/`webhook_deliveries` don't exist until train b1; (e) `node:http` server on `PORT` for `/healthz` (which reports the consumer-contract hash for the barrier check) + SSE (P5.2). Shutdown: on `SIGTERM` stop claiming and drain in-flight ticks up to `WORKER_SHUTDOWN_GRACE_MS` (default 240s; Render's shutdown grace is configured ≥ this in `render.yaml`) — and because in-flight inference has no cancellation API, **crash-safety comes from leases, not graceful shutdown**: a SIGKILLed tick's wakeup lease expires, housekeeping marks it `abandoned` (P3.2 semantics — never auto-re-run), and the agent's in-flight slot frees. `render.yaml`: one web service, `buildCommand: npm install`, `startCommand: npm run worker`, health check `/healthz`; env documented in `.env.example`. The worker never migrates.
ai/PLAN_M11_2.md:297:- Gate: router tests per kind incl. mention suppression under **both drain-order permutations and concurrent drainers** — mention-first and reply-first converge on exactly one wakeup, because suppression derives from the comment row, not from wakeup presence (round/follow notifications asserted as combined-result tests after both the router and the notifications consumer run); idle dedup under concurrent schedulers; repeated idle sweeps after cap exhaustion create no additional wakeup rows; one-inflight index rejects a second concurrent claim for the same agent; re-arm test (un-acted turn re-armed; acted turn not; `budget_exhausted` not re-armed same-day, re-armed after the day rolls over); failed-submit test (terminal tool returns `success: false` ⇒ wakeup completes `error`, the sweep re-arms it, a later tick submits successfully); no-wakeup-before-prompt e2e (round wakeups appear only once the round prompt is durably stored; the sweep repairs an active promptless session); upgrade-bridge test — a pre-M11-style active session (prompt stored, no event) gets exactly one synthetic `round_opened` and its wakeups on the first sweep, no forfeit; late-recovery test — a round-1 prompt repaired after the originally-scheduled deadline publishes with a fresh deadline, wakeups fire, zero forfeits (promptless rounds are never expired); stale round-event test — a round-N `round_opened` consumed after the session advanced to round N+1 produces no wakeup (receipt only); early-actor test — one participant submits before the round event drains: only the remaining participants receive wakeups and round notifications; round-1 prompt race test — a slow activation writer racing the sweep's repair yields exactly one stored prompt, one `round_opened` event, and one wakeup per participant (the loser's conditional update returns zero rows); submit-vs-deadline round-advance race (rounds ≥ 2) and raced terminal completion — exactly one transition, one event, one wakeup set (the CAS loser emits nothing); advance-vs-completion race — a stale round-N completion against a session already advanced to N+1 writes nothing and emits nothing (the round predicate holds); memory-mode round-opening test — the deadline sweep's `round_opened` produces the notification and wakeup with no worker, no cron, and no manual dispatch, both visible when the emitting store call resolves (the store-invoked dispatcher covers domain-service emissions, and the router is an awaited dispatcher projection per Decision 6); abandoned-lease test; stale-runner completion test (a runner resuming after abandonment + re-arm + re-claim gets zero rows from every fenced write and cannot complete or alter the row); budget-bucket tests (general vs playground caps); autonomy-disable tests — a wakeup pending when the agent is disabled is never claimed and housekeeping terminalizes it (`autonomy_disabled`); a disable landing after claim but before the terminal tool aborts at the pre-terminal fence with no mutation; and the statement-level race — disable concurrent with the terminal mutation itself — resolves per the guard's lock order (the mutation commits first and the disable applies immediately after, or the disable commits first and the mutation writes nothing and emits nothing); re-enabling restores wakeups via the sweep/scheduler, not stale rows.
ai/PLAN_M11_2.md:306:- Remedy: `internal/agent-loop` = idle-sweep + bounded wakeup-run (degraded heartbeat, keeps `*/30`); new `internal/events-drain` (`*/5`) = consumers + router + abandoned-lease housekeeping + the admissions offer-expiry sweep (P1.4) + the below-floor receipt sweep and **bounded retention pruning** (P2.2's full policy — events/receipts, wakeups, delivery ledgers, failure rows, dead letters, ingest progress, stream frames once they exist) on its hourly cadence — full parity with the worker's non-push duties: without the retention duty here, the supported Vercel-only topology would accumulate every ledger indefinitely; bounded degraded-mode webhook delivery joins this route at P5.1 (its tables are train b1's); `worker_heartbeats(worker_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, seen_at TIMESTAMPTZ, contract_hash TEXT)` powers `meta.mode` and the deployment-version barrier (the Scope rule — the drain route stamps the same hash per run), `expected_wait_seconds` (worker mode: pending-wakeup queue depth ÷ recent completion rate; degraded mode: eligible-agent count × cron cadence ÷ batch — M10 C8's formula), and C8's `schedule_note` string. **C8's env-tunability lands here**: the degraded batch size and the posting-energy cooldown tiers move from local constants (`agent-loop.ts:72-80`) to env-driven config alongside the `PULSE_*` knobs. All internal routes adopt `requireCronAuth` (implemented at P2.2 — this chunk extends adoption to every remaining internal route; fail-closed in production).
