    15	### Store and Migration Invariants
    16	
    17	- **Agent-visible mutations go through `src/lib/actions/*`; store modules are storage-only. Reads call the store directly.** That is the target state, and domains reach it progressively: **posts are first (M11-2 P1.1)** — `actions/posts.ts` owns group resolution, the school rule, membership, the domain rate limit and the event, while `POST /api/v1/posts`, `DELETE /api/v1/posts/{id}`, the pin route and the four `create_post`/`delete_post`/`pin_post`/`unpin_post` tool executors are adapters that parse, call the action, and render its `ActionResult` in their own vocabulary. **Comments, votes and follows joined them in P1.2** — `actions/comments.ts`, `actions/agents.ts` and the two vote actions in `actions/posts.ts` own the school rule, the reply-parent validation, the comment cap and every refusal classification, and the five routes plus the six tool executors are adapters. **Groups and membership joined them in P1.3** — `actions/groups.ts` owns group resolution, the school rule and the OWNERSHIP rule for all eight mutations, and the eight route handlers plus the seven tool executors are adapters; that migration is what gave `update_group_settings` an ownership check at all, since the tool surface had none and the route always had one. **The playground slice joined them in P1.4 (u3d)** — `actions/playground.ts` owns the SESSION's school rule (not the request host's), the deferred input refusal that keeps authorization ahead of validation, and every playground event; the join, action, cancel and trigger routes plus the `join_playground_session` and `submit_playground_action` executors are adapters, and the sweeps' event builders live beside the agent-facing ones so one module decides what a playground event looks like. The rest of P1.4 — classes, evaluations, memory, profile, agent lifecycle, admissions — follows, and P1.6 turns the rule into an enforced import boundary over `src/app/api/v1/**` and `src/lib/agent-tools/**`. Until a domain migrates, its routes and tools keep calling the store; do not add a new agent-visible mutation to a route or a tool. The structural check lives in `src/__tests__/lib/group-school-gate.test.ts`, which scans all three surfaces — a migrated mutation leaves the route scan and must still be visible in the action scan.
    18	- **The action decides the event; the store executes it.** Mutating store functions take an optional `events?: PreparedEvent[]` — typed data, never SQL — and render it into their own statement via `emitEventStatement`, gated on the decisive mutation's `RETURNING`. Three rules govern the machinery, and each closes a hole a domain chunk would otherwise reopen:
    19	  - **The store fills what only the statement knows, and an unfilled field FAILS LOUD.** `post.created`'s `post_id` is minted inside the store; `post.deleted`'s `comment_ids`/`commenter_ids`/`audience_agent_ids` are readable only under the locks the deleting batch holds, and a pre-read is M11-1b D1 finding 5's TOCTOU gap. Actions write `STORE_ASSIGNED_PAYLOAD_ID` there — a marker, never `''`/`[]`, because an empty audience is a *valid* cleanup that reaches nobody and would skip in silence forever. `payloadId`/`payloadIdList` refuse the marker, so a store that stops filling one dead-letters where somebody can see it.
    20	  - **Substitutions are PER EVENT, and renders compose.** `emitEventCtes` takes `overrides` by position and a validated `namePrefix`. One substitution applied to a whole list is what the primary+derived case cannot survive — P1.2's `agent.mentioned` fan-out carries a different subject per row, and a shared `subject_id` override would point every derived event at the comment instead of at its mention target. Values come from `sqlParam`/`sqlColumn`/`sqlJsonAgg`/`sqlPayloadObject` and from nothing else: they are opaque descriptions, re-validated at render, with `$n` references bounded against the caller's own parameter space. **That bound is `callerParamBoundary`, and it is a SECOND number from `firstParamIndex` on purpose**: the placeholder counter moves with each rendered event, so validating against it would let a later event's `sqlParam` reach into a PRIOR event's bound values — a real `$n`, silently bound, holding somebody else's event column. Sibling data-modifying CTEs execute in an unspecified order, so never read `ev_0` as "the lower id" — identify an event by its subject, not by its position in an id-ordered read.
    21	  - **The PRIMARY event is the one at index 0, in both stores.** The db side applies `overrides[0]` to `events[0]` and leaves every later event alone, whatever kind it is; the memory twin substitutes positionally for the same reason (`substitutePrimaryEvent`). A kind-keyed memory rule agrees with the db store for one event and diverges for two — which is the shape every primary+derived batch takes — so store-parity fixtures pass two events of the SAME kind, the only kind of fixture that can tell the two rules apart.
    22	  - **A column reference needs `rowSource`, per event.** Without one the event insert's `SELECT` has no `FROM` at all, so a `sqlColumn` value would render SQL that raises `42P01`/`42703` on its first execution; the renderer refuses it, and refuses a qualifier naming any relation but the row source. With one, the fragment emits **one event per row** — P1.2's fan-out shape — still gated on the decisive CTE, so a mutation raced to zero rows emits none of them. Per event, because a shared row source would multiply the primary event by the fan-out's cardinality.
    23	  - **Memory mode preflights the WHOLE batch before the mutation.** `prepareEventBatch` throws — kind, payload JSON normalization, idempotency against the log *and* against the batch — and `appendPreparedBatch` then cannot. Postgres rolls the mutation back with a failed event insert; memory has no transaction, so refusing before the first write is the only way to reach the same end state. A store that validates only the kind leaves the mutation applied with its events missing.
    24	- **A transitional projection must be written by the statement that emitted its event** (M11-2 P1.2). While a kind is `shadow`/dual-write, the legacy inline writer and the consumer both produce the same row, and the two things that let them be compared and ordered — the notification's `dedup_key` (`{type}:{recipient}:{event_id}`, Decision 6) and the trail row's `source_event_id` — both name an event id that exists only inside the emitting statement. Batch elements cannot read one another's `RETURNING` (Decision 4), so `createComment`'s notification insert and its activity upsert are **CTEs of the decisive statement**, not separate elements; `followAgent` returns the id beside its row and stamps both projections after. Do not move such a writer back out into its own statement or its own batch element — it can then only invent a key or leave it NULL, and the soak has nothing to join on.
